.claudinite/local/packs/canon-curation/promote.md:17:**The candidate pool is a local pack's captured knowledge — its `RULES.md` prose AND its own checks.** Two candidate kinds, both under `.claudinite/local_packs/`: a **prose rule** in a pack's `RULES.md` (a portable line never promoted before), and a **local check** (a `<pack>/*.mjs` rule module — its id, description, and failure message *are* a captured, enforceable lesson; the strongest promotion is lifting it to a **canon check**, generalized, reusing the [prose-to-checks](../../../../packs/grow_with_claudinite/skills/prose-to-checks/SKILL.md) mechanics — or to canon prose if the rule doesn't generalize to a check). **Out of scope and staying put:** a lesson deliberately placed as a **code comment at its call site** (its whole value is being at that site — lifting it into shared prose strips the context that made it right), a project's `run_daily` scripts, product source, and config. A genuinely portable rule that looks trapped in a code comment was mis-placed at extract time — leave it for a future extract pass to re-home into a pack; **never promote from product source.** You don't need to tell new from old: the dedup step below drops anything the canon already carries, so an already-promoted item simply falls out.
.claudinite/local/packs/canon-curation/promote.md:36:- **A technology with no home → mint a stub pack, in its own PR.** When a generalized, portable rule is specific to a technology that **no pack owns — not even a stub** (`android` / `ios` / `app-store-release` already count as homes; their empty `RULES.md` is a home, not a gap), do **not** force it into a near-miss doc or drop it as too specific. Mint a **fingerprinted stub pack** for that technology, reusing the pack-writing mechanics [the generate-project-instructions skill](../../../../packs/grow_with_claudinite/skills/generate-project-instructions/SKILL.md) already owns — don't re-derive them: a `packs/<tech>/` with `pack.mjs` (add the `marker`/`detect` fingerprint when the technology carries a reliable one, so the pack self-declares on future repos; leave `detect: null` when it doesn't), a `RULES.md` seeded with the lesson(s) at whatever level they distilled to, a `README.md`, and its `packs/README.md` index row. A lesson-triggered stub is a **technology** pack, so it's indexed only in `packs/README.md` and stays wholly under `packs/` — never a root `CLAUDE.md` row, which the promote write-surface gate forbids. The fingerprint is the whole point of a pack over a loose doc — it's why the *next* project on that stack self-declares instead of re-deriving. **Deliver each minted pack in its own dedicated PR**, split out from the run's ordinary lesson-promotion PR: a new pack is reviewed differently from a one-line rule addition, so it earns its own review surface. The follow-ups a fresh stub invites — enriching it from known best practices, triaging which skills it should require, spinning up dependent/aspect packs — are **not** automated here: note them for the owner and leave them to a later, deliberate pass.
.claudinite/local/packs/canon-curation/tasks/growth-promote/task.md:19:For each member named in Context, read its **local packs**. The candidate pool is a local pack's captured knowledge — its `RULES.md` **prose** AND its own **checks** (a `<pack>/*.mjs` rule module — its id, description, and failure message *are* a captured, enforceable lesson; the strongest promotion lifts it to a **canon check**, generalized, reusing the [prose-to-checks](../../../../../../packs/grow_with_claudinite/skills/prose-to-checks/SKILL.md) mechanics — or to canon prose when it doesn't generalize to a check). **Out of scope, staying put:** a lesson deliberately placed as a **code comment at its call site**, a project's task scripts, product source, and config. Never promote from product source. You don't need to tell new from old — the dedup step drops anything the canon already carries. No barrier holds tonight's extracts: read whatever is already **merged** on each member's default branch; anything landing mid-run is tomorrow's input.
.claudinite/local/packs/canon-curation/tasks/growth-promote/task.md:33:- **A technology with no home → mint a fingerprinted stub pack, in its own PR**, reusing the pack-writing mechanics [the generate-project-instructions skill](../../../../../../packs/grow_with_claudinite/skills/generate-project-instructions/SKILL.md) owns — `packs/<tech>/` with `pack.mjs` (add the `marker`/`detect` fingerprint when the technology carries a reliable one, else `detect: null`), a `RULES.md` seeded from the lesson, a `README.md`, and its `packs/README.md` index row. A minted pack ships in its **own** PR, split from the run's lesson-promotion PR (a new pack earns its own review surface). Never mint from imagination and never park an empty one to fill later — a pack minted here is populated from the *real, portable lesson* that justified it.
packs/grow_with_claudinite/tasks/growth-discover-packs/task.md:33:For each candidate, author a populated pack under `.claudinite/local/packs/<name>/`, distilled from **how this project actually works** — never from imagination. Apply the [generate-project-instructions](../../skills/generate-project-instructions/SKILL.md) method (don't re-derive it): descend the promotion ladder — a rule a deterministic check can carry becomes the **check plus a see-it-fail fixture** (fires on a violating input, quiet on a clean one), a procedure with a nameable trigger becomes a skill, and only signature-less judgment lands as `RULES.md` prose. Ground and cite every rule in the project's real files; **never pad**, and **never open an empty stub to fill later**. Write the pack files (`RULES.md`, `pack.mjs`, `README.md`), register it, and **declare it** in the repo's `.claudinite-checks.json` so it actually activates.
--- checks.json packs ---
{
  "packs": [
    "basics",
    "github-actions",
    {
      "id": "grow_with_claudinite",
      "config": {
        "retention_days": 10,
        "pack_paths": [".claudinite/local/packs", "packs"]
      },
      "answers": {
        "retention": "10 days — the recommended rethink window before a captured conversation log is pruned."
      }
    },
    "local/claudinite",
    {
      "id": "local/canon-curation",
      "accept": [
        {
          "rule": "file-placement",
          "path": ".claudinite/local/packs/canon-curation/",
          "reason": "the canon home's curation local pack deliberately shares the checks/ engine lib (findings, lines, context) — the same named cross-cutting concern as the packs/ acceptance; in the home checkout the engine is the repo's own tracked tree, not a consumer mount, so the reach is inherent"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "barriers",
      "answers": {
        "goals": "Keep extending.md's engine/content boundary structural: core (checks/, migrations/, mount/, routines/, .github/, the top-level docs, and the shared engine modules under packs/ and skills/) must never name a specific pack or skill — packs and skills are discovered content, and a core file citing one couples the engine to its content. One rule per namespace (packs/*, skills/*); reviewed inherent couplings live in each rule's except."
      },
      "config": {
        "rules": [
          {
            "from": [
              "engine",
              "migrations",
              "routines",
              ".github",
              "README.md",
              "bootstrap.md",
              "consumer-safe-changes.md",
              "extending.md"
            ],
            "to": "packs/*",
            "matchNames": true,
            "matchUniqueFilenames": false,
            "reason": "core is the engine that runs pack content — it must not name the specific packs it runs (extending.md)",
            "except": [
              {
                "path": ".github/workflows/README.md",
                "to": [
                  "packs/chrome-extension",
                  "packs/chrome-extension-release"
                ],
                "reason": "decouple(release-infra): the core .github/ README documents the chrome-extension-release plumbing being vendored out of core (#276)"
              },
              {
                "path": ".github/workflows/chrome-extension-daily-release.yml",
                "to": [
                  "packs/chrome-extension",
                  "packs/chrome-extension-release",
                  "packs/github-actions"
                ],
                "reason": "decouple(release-infra): chrome-extension-release reusable release infra hosted in the core .github/ tree; being vendored out (#276)"
              },
              {
                "path": ".github/workflows/chrome-extension-publish-store.yml",
                "to": [
                  "packs/chrome-extension",
                  "packs/chrome-extension-release"
                ],
                "reason": "decouple(release-infra): chrome-extension-release reusable release infra hosted in the core .github/ tree; being vendored out (#276)"
              },
              {
                "path": ".github/workflows/chrome-extension-release.yml",
                "to": [
                  "packs/chrome-extension",
                  "packs/chrome-extension-release"
                ],
                "reason": "decouple(release-infra): chrome-extension-release reusable release infra hosted in the core .github/ tree; being vendored out (#276)"
              },
              {
                "path": ".github/actions/read-release-config/read-config.mjs",
                "to": [
                  "packs/chrome-extension-release"
                ],
                "reason": "decouple(release-infra): a chrome-release composite action in the core .github/ tree; being vendored out (#276)"
              },
              {
                "path": "migrations/active_migrations/2026-07-12-tidy-repo-seed.mjs",
                "to": [
                  "packs/tidy-repo"
                ],
                "reason": "inherent: a one-time seed migration necessarily names the single pack it seeds; it self-retires once the fleet converges"
              },
              {
                "path": "migrations/active_migrations/2026-07-13-chrome-release-vendoring.mjs",
                "to": [
                  "packs/chrome-extension-release"
                ],
                "reason": "inherent: a one-time vendoring migration necessarily names the pack whose plumbing it relocates into consumers; it self-retires once the fleet converges"
              },
              {
                "path": "extending.md",
                "to": [
                  "packs/grow_with_claudinite"
                ],
                "reason": "inherent(#385): the engine map documents where the adoption-interview machinery lives now that it is bundled in the adoption skill"
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      }
    }
  ],
  "accept": [
    {
      "rule": "file-placement",
      "path": "packs/",
      "reason": "pack check modules deliberately share the checks/ engine lib — a named cross-cutting concern per skills/file-placement/SKILL.md; packs are organized by pack, not by dependency"
    },
    {
      "rule": "file-placement",
      "path": "skills/",
      "reason": "a skill's check-the-work modules deliberately share the checks/ engine lib (findings.mjs) — the same named cross-cutting concern as the packs/ acceptance; checks are co-located with the skill whose action they validate, not organized by dependency"
    },
    {
      "rule": "file-placement",
      "path": "routines/fleet/plan.mjs",
      "reason": "the core planner deliberately imports the pack-discovery registry (engine/pack_loader/pack-registry.mjs — core machinery per extending.md, not a specific pack) to enumerate each repo's run_daily tasks; discovery lives under packs/ and the planner under routines/, so the cross-tree reach is inherent and correct, not a misplacement"
    },
    {
      "rule": "file-placement",
      "path": "routines/fleet/signals.mjs",
      "reason": "the member-declaration read deliberately imports the one shared id-extractor (packEntryId in engine/pack_loader/pack-registry.mjs — core machinery per extending.md, not a specific pack) so the fleet's activePacks normalization and the engine can never drift on the declaration forms; the cross-tree reach is inherent, the same as the planner's registry import"
    },
    {
      "rule": "file-placement",
      "path": "engine/checks/helpers/repo-context.mjs",
      "reason": "the engine's file-set builder deliberately imports the consumer-layout constant SHARED_SUBDIR (beside LOCAL_PACKS_SUBDIR in engine/pack_loader/pack-registry.mjs — core machinery per extending.md, not a specific pack) so the shared-mount sweep exclusion and the vendor writer share one definition; the cross-tree reach is inherent, not a misplacement"
    },
    {
      "rule": "file-placement",
      "path": "migrations/registry.mjs",
      "reason": "the canon-side migration registry deliberately builds on the vendored sync surface in the engine lib (engine/checks/helpers/active-migrations.mjs — the home pack-independence mandates, since pack checks consult migrationActive and packs import only the engine surface); one definition, re-exported for canon callers, so the cross-tree reach is inherent, not a misplacement"
    },
    {
      "rule": "file-placement",
      "path": "vendoring/compute-vendor-set.mjs",
      "reason": "the vendor-set computation deliberately imports the import-surface facts from the engine lib (engine/checks/helpers/module-imports.mjs — the one home the pack-independence check judges against), so \"what a pack may import\" and \"what a vendor set must carry\" share one definition; the cross-tree reach is inherent, not a misplacement"
    }
  ],
  "taskScheduler": {
    "dailyHour": 4,
    "weeklyDay": "Sun",
    "monthlyDay": 1
  }
}

